🧠 Pehle Senior Developer ki tarah socho

Suppose requirement aayi:

"Company ke employees ke liye login system banao."

Junior immediately sochta hai:

login API bana do

Senior pehle questions poochega:

1. User kaun hai?
2. User ki identity kaise verify hogi?
3. Password database mein kaise store hoga?
4. Login ke baad server ko kaise pata chalega ki
   request kis user ki hai?
5. User ko har request par password dena padega?
6. Employee aur Admin ko same access milega?
7. User logout kaise karega?
8. Token expire hone par kya hoga?
9. Password leak ho gaya to?
10. Third-party Google login chahiye to?

Ye thought process hi tumhe junior se strong backend developer banayega.

Phase 1 — Authentication vs Authorization

Sabse pehle ye difference crystal clear:

Authentication

"Tum kaun ho?"

Example:

email + password
        ↓
   Login
        ↓
"Vishal hai"
Authorization

"Tumhe kya karne ki permission hai?"

Example:

Vishal = Employee

GET /profile       ✅
GET /users         ❌
DELETE /users/10   ❌

Admin:

GET /profile       ✅
GET /users         ✅
DELETE /users/10   ✅
Real-life analogy

Office building:

Security Guard
      ↓
"ID dikhao"
      ↓
Authentication
      ↓
"Ye employee Vishal hai"
      ↓
Authorization
      ↓
"Kya Vishal ko server room mein jaane ki permission hai?"
Phase 2 — Password Hashing

Ab problem:

Database mein ye kabhi nahi:

email: vishal@gmail.com
password: 123456

Agar database leak ho gaya:

Attacker → passwords → all accounts compromised

Isliye:

123456
   ↓
Hashing
   ↓
$2b$10$....

Database mein hash store hoga.

Important concept:

Hashing ≠ Encryption

Encryption reversible hoti hai:

data → encrypt → encrypted
encrypted → decrypt → data

Hashing:

password → hash

normally original password ko wapas nahi nikala jata.

Phase 3 — bcrypt

Ab hum bcrypt use karenge.

Registration:

POST /auth/register

email
password
name

Backend:

password
   ↓
bcrypt.hash()
   ↓
passwordHash
   ↓
MongoDB

Login:

password entered
       ↓
bcrypt.compare()
       ↓
stored hash
       ↓
true / false
Important senior-level question

Hash ko decrypt karke password kyun nahi nikalte?

Because password authentication ka purpose password recover karna nahi, password verify karna hai.

Phase 4 — JWT

Login successful:

email + password
       ↓
   authenticate
       ↓
      JWT
       ↓
    client

JWT ke andar generally claims hote hain, e.g.:

userId
role
expiry

Client next request mein token bhejega:

Authorization: Bearer <token>

Backend:

Request
   ↓
JWT verify
   ↓
user identify
   ↓
request continue
Phase 5 — Access Token

Access token ka purpose:

Current API requests ko authenticate karna.

Example:

POST /auth/login
        ↓
Access Token
        ↓
GET /users/me
        ↓
GET /tasks
        ↓
POST /tasks

Access token usually short-lived rakha jata hai.

Why?

Suppose token steal ho gaya.

Agar:

token expiry = 15 minutes

to attacker ke paas limited time hai.

Phase 6 — Refresh Token

Ab real-world problem:

Access token expire ho gaya.

User ko har 15 minute baad:

email
password

enter karwana bad UX hai.

Solution:

Access Token
     ↓
short life

and

Refresh Token
     ↓
longer life

Flow:

LOGIN
  ↓
Access Token + Refresh Token
  ↓
Access token expires
  ↓
Refresh Token
  ↓
New Access Token
  ↓
User continues working
Senior question:

Do tokens kyun?

Because:

Security + User Experience

ko balance karna hai.

Phase 7 — Cookies

Ab question:

Refresh token client mein kahan rakhen?

Web applications mein secure cookie ek common approach hai.

Example concept:

Set-Cookie:
refreshToken=....
HttpOnly
Secure
SameSite
HttpOnly

JavaScript directly cookie read nahi kar sakta.

Ye XSS risk ko reduce karne mein useful hai.

Secure

Cookie HTTPS par bheji jaye.

SameSite

Cross-site request behavior control karne mein help karta hai.

Phase 8 — Sessions

Ab ek important architectural decision.

Authentication ke do common approaches:

Session-based

vs

Token-based
Session

Server:

sessionId → user data

Client:

session cookie

Request:

Cookie
 ↓
Server
 ↓
Session lookup
 ↓
User
JWT
Client
 ↓
JWT
 ↓
Server verifies token
 ↓
User

Hum project mein dono concepts implement/compare karenge, taaki interview mein sirf definition nahi, actual trade-off explain kar sako.

Phase 9 — RBAC

Ab maan lo:

Admin
Manager
Employee

Har user ko same access nahi milna chahiye.

Example:

Admin
 ├── create user
 ├── delete user
 ├── update role
 └── view all users

Manager
 ├── view team
 └── manage team tasks

Employee
 ├── view own profile
 └── manage own tasks

Ye hai:

Role-Based Access Control

Flow:

Request
 ↓
Authentication
 ↓
Who are you?
 ↓
Authorization
 ↓
What is your role?
 ↓
Allowed?
 ↓
Controller
Phase 10 — Permissions

RBAC ko aur powerful banayenge.

Instead of:

if role === "admin"

hum permissions define kar sakte hain:

users:read
users:create
users:update
users:delete

tasks:read
tasks:create
tasks:update
tasks:delete

Then:

Admin
→ users:read
→ users:create
→ users:update
→ users:delete

Manager
→ users:read
→ tasks:read
→ tasks:create
→ tasks:update

Employee
→ tasks:read
→ tasks:create

Ab system zyada flexible ho gaya.

